<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch10_P01_Follow_with_numbers</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the proof that \(2 = 1\) from the example above, in numbered lines.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Suppose that \(a = b\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Then \(a^2 = ab\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>\(a^2 - b^2 = ab - b^2\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>\((a - b)(a + b) = b(a - b)\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>\(a + b = b\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>\(b + b = b\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>\(2b = b\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>\(2 = 1\)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A correct step never leads from true lines to a false one. So if you choose values that make line 1 true, the first line that is false for your values cannot follow from the lines before it.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Take \(a = b = 3\). Which is the first line that is false?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Now take \(a = b = 0\). Which is the first line that is false?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
<li><p>Line 1 is the assumption. Which of lines 2 to 8 do not follow from the lines before them? Enter the set of line numbers in curly brackets, for example <code>{2, 3}</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
<li><p>Why does each of these lines not follow? (Optional: this box is not marked and not saved.)</p>
<div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A correct step never leads from true lines to a false one. So follow the proof with numbers that make line 1 true, and look for the first line that becomes false: the step to that line cannot be valid.</p>
<p>With \(a = b = 3\), the two sides of each line are:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Line</th><th>Left side</th><th>Right side</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>9</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>6</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>6</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The first false line is line 5, so line 5 does not follow from lines 1 to 4. It divides both sides of line 4 by \(a - b\), which is 0 because \(a = b\). From \(xy = xz\) we may conclude \(y = z\) only when \(x \neq 0\).</p>
<p>With \(a = b = 0\), every line from 1 to 7 reads \(0 = 0\), which is true, and line 8 says \(2 = 1\). So line 8 does not follow from line 7: it divides both sides of \(2b = b\) by \(b\), which is 0 here, and nothing in the proof says that \(b \neq 0\).</p>
<p>So the lines that do not follow are 5 and 8. Lines 6 and 7 are false when \(a = b = 3\), but each follows correctly from the line before it: line 6 replaces \(a\) by \(b\), and line 7 collects \(b + b = 2b\). Truth and validity are different things: a valid step can lead from a false line to another false line. A false proof can contain more than one invalid step, and one can hide behind another.</p>
<p>A reason you might have written: <q>Line 5 divides both sides by \(a - b\), which is 0; line 8 divides both sides by \(b\), which might be 0.</q></p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
